+# BH solver application-licence proposal
+
+Date: 2026-09-11. Status: **PROPOSED TERMS AND REVIEW QUESTIONS; NOT A LICENCE**.
+
+The owner selected non-commercial, source-available licensing for the owned code
+of the **BH solver (BH solver)**, with Qt/PySide on the LGPLv3 route.
+The future industry stream has the provisional name **Dynamic Industry Virtual
+Analysis (future release)**. These names convey no release readiness, capability or approval.
+The owner also excludes commercially licensed libraries at this stage and may
+keep separately owned proprietary packages closed.
+
+This proposal records that intent for review. It does not replace the current
+[Apache-2.0 LICENSE](../../LICENSE) or `pyproject.toml` licence metadata, grant new
+rights, adopt Qt, or settle the wording of an application licence. The approved
+DW0 boundary work and neutral DW1 implementation can proceed independently of
+this publication decision. Cross-references: [L-01](DECISION_POINTS.md),
+[dependency register](../DEPENDENCY_REGISTER.md), and
+[governed release](../INDUSTRY_DEVELOPMENT_STREAM.md).
+
+## 1. Proposed scope and terminology
+
+Use **source-available**, rather than **OSI open-source**, for the proposed BH
+application licence. Public source access alone does not meet the Open Source
+Definition; a restriction on business use conflicts with its field-of-endeavour
+criterion. [OSI Open Source Definition, especially Sections 1 and 6](https://opensource.org/osd).
+
+| Material | Proposed treatment |
+|---|---|
+| BH application code that the owner has authority to license | A separately reviewed non-commercial source-available application licence; exact file/version coverage recorded before adoption |
+| Existing Apache-licensed code, including any received third-party contributions | Preserve applicable grants and notices; investigate the transition described below |
+| Qt, PySide, Shiboken and their modifications | Applicable upstream licences, including LGPLv3 where available; no BH non-commercial restriction imposed on those components |
+| Other dependencies, scientific papers, datasets, model weights and dictionaries | Their own code/data/service terms and redistribution records; no blanket relicensing |
+| Separately owned closed packages and future future release code | Separate inventory and terms; ownership and integration rights reviewed individually |
+| User cases, results, transcripts and other user material | No new ownership, upload or reuse rights created by this proposal |
+
+Working interpretation of “no commercially licensed libraries”: do not acquire
+or adopt dependencies requiring proprietary/commercial licence terms. Existing
+permissive dependencies do not become excluded merely because their licences
+also permit commercial use. This interpretation leaves the owner's selected
+LGPLv3 route intact; exact components still need adoption review.
+
+## 2. Application terms to prepare
+
+Suggested drafting basis: permit inspection, local execution, modification and
+redistribution of the covered BH source and binaries for defined non-commercial
+purposes, retaining attribution and licence notices. A future derivative-work
+clause should specify whether distributed BH modifications must include their
+preferred source form and retain the same non-commercial terms. These are
+recommendations for owner review, not permissions supplied by this document.
+
+The largest unresolved issue is what **commercial use** means. Do not infer it
+from whether the download is free, a user is employed, or an organisation makes a
+profit. Two viable drafting directions remain:
+
+| Option | Consequence |
+|---|---|
+| Purpose-based restriction, recommended for drafting | Exclude use materially directed at commercial advantage, including paid consulting, internal production/design work and hosted paid services; expressly define education, research and evaluation exceptions |
+| Explicit permitted-use categories | List permitted personal, teaching and specified research uses; separately define all organisational evaluation permissions. Easier to illustrate, but boundary cases may require more exceptions |
+
+Before preparing operative text, record the owner's choices for these examples:
+
+- Unpaid personal study, classroom teaching and degree research: proposed allowed.
+- Grant-funded academic work, industry-sponsored research, government and charity
+  work: decide by purpose and funding relationship; “research” is insufficient.
+- A business evaluating suitability without operational use: decide whether a
+  bounded evaluation exception exists and what it permits.
+- Paid engineering advice, internal commercial R&D, production decisions, resale,
+  paid hosting, support services and training: define which activities are
+  prohibited and whether any separate permission route exists.
+- Cost-recovery distribution, donations and tuition-funded courses: decide
+  expressly to avoid treating all payment as the same activity.
+
+Also specify licence versioning, attribution, modified-version identification,
+patent treatment, termination/cure, warranty/liability language and jurisdiction
+with appropriate legal review. Avoid a name or metadata identifier implying an
+OSI-approved licence. No commercial exception or future future release entitlement is
+approved by this proposal.
+
+## 3. Preserve LGPL rights in the combined application
+
+LGPLv3 Section 4 permits combined-work terms subject to conditions. Those terms
+must preserve library modification and reverse engineering for debugging those
+modifications. Required items include prominent library notices, GPLv3 and LGPLv3
+texts, and applicable runtime copyright notices. Section 4(d) offers a suitable
+shared-library mechanism or the prescribed source/application-code relinking
+route; Section 4(e) requires installation information when its GPLv3 conditions
+apply. Draft BH restrictions expressly around owned application code, with
+necessary third-party and library-rights exceptions. Do not restrict recipients'
+commercial rights in LGPL-covered components. This is a scoped interpretation
+to review against the final terms and payload.
+[LGPLv3 text, Sections 0 and 4, as published by Qt](https://doc.qt.io/qt-6/lgpl.html).
+
+Qt's guidance additionally stresses corresponding library source, including
+modifications, recipient replacement/relinking rights, and compatibility of
+distribution terms. Publishing BH source or selecting dynamic linking does not
+by itself demonstrate compliance. For the macOS-first package, retain a concrete
+source-delivery method and test library replacement and execution with the chosen
+packaging/signing arrangement. Assess store terms if a store is selected.
+[Qt LGPL obligations](https://www.qt.io/development/open-source-lgpl-obligations).
+
+Some Qt modules use GPL rather than LGPL in the open-source offering. Inventory
+the exact modules, plugins, tools, binaries and third-party contents; exclude
+components incompatible with the selected application terms rather than treating
+the whole Qt installation as approved. Review tools and their generated output
+separately. [Qt licensing and SBOM documentation](https://doc.qt.io/qt-6/licensing.html).
+Qt for Python also contains separately licensed third-party material requiring
+component-specific treatment. [Qt for Python licences](https://doc.qt.io/qtforpython-6/licenses.html).
+
+## 4. Existing Apache terms, contributors and closed packages
+
+The repository currently supplies Apache-2.0. Its copyright grant is perpetual
+and irrevocable subject to its terms; Section 4 permits different terms for
+modifications or a derivative work as a whole while retaining its conditions.
+Consequently, a future BH licence cannot simply withdraw compliant recipients'
+existing Apache rights in previously supplied code. Section 5 also addresses
+contribution terms. [Apache License 2.0, Sections 2, 4 and 5](https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0).
+
+Before changing the root licence or publishing a restricted release, establish:
+
+1. Which versions/files have been distributed, to whom or through which public
+   channels, and under what terms. An uncommitted local repository does not prove
+   that earlier copies were never supplied elsewhere.
+2. Copyright ownership and permissions for each covered portion, including
+   collaborators, employer/university obligations and imported or generated code.
+   Do not assume the project owner can relicense every contribution.
+3. The exact prospective licence boundary, retained Apache notices and metadata
+   changes. Record why restrictions can apply to new owned material and which
+   earlier material remains reusable under its existing terms.
+4. A contribution policy before accepting outside work: either explicit rights
+   supporting both BH and future future release licensing, or a clearly limited inbound
+   licence. Contributors must understand any intended proprietary reuse.
+
+Recommend keeping closed packages behind declared neutral ports with separate
+source and dependency inventories. This improves reviewability; a plugin name,
+dynamic import or separate process is not a legal exemption. Review copied code,
+library modifications, linking and redistribution facts. Proprietary status does
+not excuse LGPL obligations, permit redistribution of third-party proprietary
+data, or automatically authorise future release to use non-commercial BH contributions.
+No closed dependency is adopted by this proposal.
+
+## 5. Intended use and adoption record
+
+Retain the existing concept-development warning: BH solver is not approved for
+industrial, safety-critical, plant-operation or regulatory reliance. State the
+actual verification boundary and preserve separate scientific validity states.
+This warning describes scientific status. It is neither a replacement for licence
+terms nor evidence of legal enforceability, regulatory compliance or scientific
+certification. future release remains separately governed and unqualified at this stage.
+
+Adoption requires an owner-approved application-licence draft, resolved ownership
+and prior-distribution questions, and a consistent file/metadata/notice update.
+Assign a named compliance reviewer and retain a dated decision. Actual Qt
+adoption and later distribution additionally require versioned payload evidence
+under DW2/DW9. Approval of a licence route is not release compliance clearance.
+
+## 6. Change record — Appendix A
+
+- **Objective and authority:** prepare the separate application-licence proposal
+  requested in the owner's 2026-09-11 L-01 clarification. References: DW0/L-01,
+  ADR-006/010 and the action plan's Sections 12, 17 and Appendix A.
+- **Scope and boundaries:** this new document only. No application, kernel,
+  worker, schema, persistence, licence file, dependency or scientific-model change.
+- **Assumptions and constraints:** owner intent is confirmed; ownership,
+  publication history, exact terms and payload compliance remain unverified.
+- **Evidence:** local `LICENSE`, `pyproject.toml`, dependency register, DW0 licence
+  brief/change record and Appendix A read; primary sources linked above checked
+  on 2026-09-11. GNU's website did not load; the LGPL text was inspected in Qt's
+  official publication instead. No third-party commentary supplies legal claims.
+- **Design and alternatives:** separate intended BH restrictions from upstream
+  licences and record outstanding definitions. A blanket LGPL licence would not
+  implement the selected non-commercial intent; immediate replacement of Apache
+  metadata would misstate the unresolved transition. The tradeoff is a further
+  explicit publication decision.
+- **Impact:** documentation only; no legal grant, migration, dependency adoption,
+  privacy permission, scientific approval or commercial-library purchase.
+- **Checks:** local Markdown links and required scope markers checked using
+  `.venv/bin/python` with a read-only inline script; all local targets resolved
+  and all scope assertions passed after normalising whitespace in the checker
+  (its first literal-name check failed on the wrapped future release name). `LICENSE` and
+  `pyproject.toml` were not edited
+  by this work. No software tests run because executable behaviour is unchanged;
+  no ownership audit, complete licence review or installer compliance test run.
+- **Risk, rollback and limitations:** the proposal could be mistaken for operative
+  terms; its status and current Apache boundary are explicit. Rollback is removal
+  of this document after checking for subsequent edits. No unrelated files changed
+  by this subtask. Legal suitability remains to be assessed on concrete terms.
+- **Open owner decisions:** commercial-use cases and exceptions; derivative-source
+  policy; ownership/distribution record; contribution and future release reuse rights;
+  final drafting/review responsibility. These are publication decisions, not a
+  reason to repeat the already approved DW0 gate or stop neutral DW1 work.
